00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.090
community to learn more
about Google Cloud.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.550
JON FOUST: And
today, we're going

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.002
to be talking about SAP HANA.

00:04:09.002 --> 00:04:11.210
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, at a high
level, what is SAP HANA?

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:12.340
I actually have no idea.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.090
LUCIA SUBATIN: It's a
data and application

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:19.013
platform that has an in-memory
column-oriented database in it.

00:04:19.013 --> 00:04:20.180
MARK MANDEL: That was a lot.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.472
LUCIA SUBATIN: And I can go on.

00:04:21.472 --> 00:04:22.640
I go on for hours.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.598
MARK MANDEL: So maybe
break that down a little,

00:04:24.598 --> 00:04:25.620
just like a touch.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.000
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well, it's the
platform, which we run our SAP

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.250
business applications.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:30.920
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.090
KEVIN NELSON: I think
one of the key benefits

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.850
that HANA provides with this
in-memory database is being

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.320
able to handle large enterprise
transactions with very, very

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.410
tight time constraints.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.530
So you're able to handle
the day-to-day business

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.450
needs, guaranteeing
that you're not

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.920
going to go out and hit disk.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.050
So you get really good latency.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:48.890
JON FOUST: Yeah.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:50.000
MARK MANDEL: You said something
interesting in there, too.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.820
You said it's both a data
and an application layer.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.430
LUCIA SUBATIN: Yes, so
traditionally, our ERP

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.680
applications, for
example, where customers

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.330
run their operations and all of
their business related stuff,

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:04.140
ran on any database.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.160
So when those business
applications started running,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.980
they were database agnostic.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:09.980
And that was on purpose.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.490
But with time, we realized that
our customers and businesses,

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.750
they really value their data.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.840
And then you also
understand that as Google.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.080
And we found that we
needed to provide them

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.230
with a better way
of gaining real time

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.280
insights about their business.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.640
So for example, when I
used to do consulting,

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.900
one thing that was pretty usual
is for somebody like a CFO

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.090
to have to wait for
a week before they

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.630
could answer the question of
how their business finance is

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:39.590
going.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:42.500
And we found that we
had to solve that,

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:45.260
and that was not something
that was acceptable anymore.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.260
So we came up with this
platform that being in memory

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.470
and having columnar capabilities
and having the ability

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:56.030
to run advanced analytics allows
these applications to tap on

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.020
data really, really fast.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.480
So for that case,
this specific example

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.640
I was talking about of the
week running report, that

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.820
turned into less
than five minutes,

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.520
to give you an example
of why HANA is so

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.467
important to our applications.

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:13.550
JON FOUST: Sounds amazing.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:17.122
I'm curious, what can we
expect from SAP and GCP?

00:06:17.122 --> 00:06:18.830
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well,
we can expect a lot.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.020
In terms of what
we have been doing

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:24.890
with Kevin and some other
advocates in your team,

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.340
we have been trying to bring
our communities together,

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.470
our developer
communities together.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.410
The developer community
at SAP is huge,

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.080
and in the different roles,
we have people who really

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.713
understand the enterprise.

00:06:37.713 --> 00:06:38.630
And that's what we do.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.270
We are leaders in
enterprise applications.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.210
And at the same time, our
customers need agility.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:48.780
And they need a reliable place
to run these applications.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.530
So what we are trying to do
is bring the Google developers

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:54.530
in the advocate together with
the enterprise developers

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.128
and cross pollinate knowledge.

00:06:56.128 --> 00:06:57.420
JON FOUST: That sounds amazing.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.040
So which products would you
say that SAP HANA and GCP

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:01.873
specifically target?

00:07:01.873 --> 00:07:03.873
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well, we
are talking about always

00:07:03.873 --> 00:07:05.010
the business applications.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.580
So all of our
business suites, like

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.450
S/4HANA, which is the
evolution of the ERP

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:14.540
and the C/4HANA, which
is for like a CRM.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:16.490
MARK MANDEL: What's an
ERP and a CRM for those

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:17.260
who don't know those terms?

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:18.177
LUCIA SUBATIN: Oh, OK.

00:07:18.177 --> 00:07:19.950
That's a very good question.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.770
That's very natural
to me, so thank you

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.260
for pointing that out.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:26.030
An ERP system is Enterprise
Resource Planning.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.740
That's what it stands for.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:32.510
And what it does is control all
of different areas or business

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:33.480
areas in the company.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.110
So for example, you will
have there all the logistics,

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:39.080
the procurement,
the finance, HR,

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:43.280
everything that a company needs
to keep track of and assist

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:46.580
them in terms of transactional
and then analytics.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.500
That is what an ERP system does.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:49.630
It keeps track of it.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.970
And otherwise, you would have
to have Excel spreadsheets,

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.430
and nobody wants that
to run their business.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.642
Yes, that's why you're laughing.

00:07:57.642 --> 00:07:58.350
You should laugh.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.650
MARK MANDEL: I'm not saying I've
done that, but I've done that.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:01.400
JON FOUST: Me too.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:03.358
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well, if
it's just for yourself,

00:08:03.358 --> 00:08:04.040
it makes sense.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.930
Or if you have a
very small business,

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.470
that maybe makes sense.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.220
But if you are talking
about a global company,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.520
you need to really have
a system behind that.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.730
So what we did with
ERP is have the logics

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:19.710
there for all of the
business areas in a company.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.710
KEVIN NELSON: Let me plug
SAP a little bit here.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.950
SAP does this type of software
for most of the biggest

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:26.118
companies in the world.

00:08:26.118 --> 00:08:28.160
So they've had to deal
with the unique challenges

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.920
of every single business and
how to manage those resources

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.590
at vast, vast scale.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:34.840
LUCIA SUBATIN: I did not
have those facts super clear.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:37.350
But we have at least 25
different industries.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.510
And I can talk about what
I implemented myself.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.240
So I've been at an auto company.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.240
I've been at a huge retail
company that has supermarkets

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:45.690
all over the world.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:50.480
I've been at a company that
does over 80% of the chocolate

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:51.895
in the world, which is amazing.

00:08:51.895 --> 00:08:54.020
KEVIN NELSON: Your particular
favorite place to be.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:55.040
LUCIA SUBATIN: Exactly, yes.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.090
That's why I remember it.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:59.330
So I've even implemented
SAP at an airline.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.690
So you also need to keep track
of the maintenance of planes.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.730
So that is some of the
things that SAP does.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:05.480
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.490
Going back to what
Jon was talking about,

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.470
what unique advantages
do people have

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.990
running SAP on Google Cloud?

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.508
