00:09:13.508 --> 00:09:14.550
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well, yes.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.490
Because traditionally,
we were having

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.010
all these systems on-premise.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:23.840
And our customers are not
interested in doing data center

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:25.550
related stuff.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:28.010
And you guys at Google
are great at that.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:28.843
You're leaders and--

00:09:28.843 --> 00:09:30.052
MARK MANDEL: We do all right.

00:09:30.052 --> 00:09:30.775
Thanks, yes.

00:09:30.775 --> 00:09:31.998
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:31.998 --> 00:09:34.040
KEVIN NELSON: Yeah, the
world's moving to a place

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.380
where in order to be
successful, every company has

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.080
to do what they are best at.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.840
And SAP is fantastic at
developing and deploying

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.420
these massive
enterprise applications.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:47.540
Google's fantastic at
infrastructure, security,

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:50.697
power management, all the things
about keeping hardware running

00:09:50.697 --> 00:09:53.030
and making sure that the
application has a platform that

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:54.590
can be fast, that
can be low latency,

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.702
that can be successful,
that can be safe.

00:09:56.702 --> 00:09:58.160
LUCIA SUBATIN: In
that way, we have

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.610
business minding their own
business, I always say.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.410
Then also, there's the
piece that's not minor,

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:06.390
which is being able to scale.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:08.720
So if you have your own data
center, you can't scale,

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.590
but you guys provide a lot of
options for scaling and scaling

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:13.377
reliably.

00:10:13.377 --> 00:10:14.960
And that is very
important when you're

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:16.002
running things like HANA.

00:10:16.002 --> 00:10:17.915
HANA, being an
in-memory platform,

00:10:17.915 --> 00:10:19.640
it needs a lot of RAM.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:23.990
So it needs proper hardware,
and we did a joint effort

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.010
to certify your hardware.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:29.150
So that's also why it's a good
place to run our applications

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:30.548
and specifically, HANA.

00:10:30.548 --> 00:10:31.340
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:33.050
I'm a very big hardware guy--

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.610
RAM, SSDs, everything.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:35.770
LUCIA SUBATIN: Yes.

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.670
JON FOUST: So definitely at
the top of my list for anything

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:38.390
that I build.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:39.720
So I have a question.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.323
Focus more about BigQuery and
immigration with SAP HANA.

00:10:42.323 --> 00:10:43.990
LUCIA SUBATIN: OK,
yes, that's something

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:46.330
that we have been
showcasing a lot.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:51.100
So we have this capability of
integrating BigQuery into HANA

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:53.775
by using smart data adapter.

00:10:53.775 --> 00:10:55.150
That is something
that technology

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:58.660
that we have in HANA that allows
us to simply go and tap and do

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.900
queries on BigQuery's
huge data sets.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.050
So generally, we
would go and tap

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:10.030
into what would be probably
cold data for a HANA system

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:12.520
and run the aggregations
and whatever

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:14.110
we need to do in BigQuery.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.480
And then move it over
to HANA to integrate it

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:17.673
with warm or hot data.

00:11:17.673 --> 00:11:19.840
KEVIN NELSON: Yeah, this
really is a key capability.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.960
Because one of the constraints
of in-memory database

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:24.970
is obviously, businesses
have more data

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:27.260
than can fit in physical memory.

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:30.250
So having it running in HANA
in this column database,

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:31.942
everything is fast, low latency.

00:11:31.942 --> 00:11:34.150
But what do you do with last
year's data and the data

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:36.310
that you want to run
historical reports on?

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.070
So once that data is
offloaded into BigQuery, now

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:42.098
you can integrate that into
queries directly within HANA.

00:11:42.098 --> 00:11:44.140
It's sitting there, but
then gaining the benefits

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:46.000
of Google's massive
scale for doing

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.620
these massive petabyte scale
queries in a matter of minutes.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.190
So it provides a
unique capability

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.560
for looking at historical
data and joining data

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.850
across different systems.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:56.517
MARK MANDEL: That
probably segues nicely

00:11:56.517 --> 00:11:59.572
into what sort of workloads
we run on SAP HANA.

00:11:59.572 --> 00:12:01.030
I think you've
mentioned analytics,

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:02.830
but now we're talking
about integrating BigQuery.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:04.663
And there seems like
there's a lot of things

00:12:04.663 --> 00:12:05.740
that this could do.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.810
Where do you see its
sweet spot really being?

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:08.380
LUCIA SUBATIN:
Actually, what you're

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:10.240
describing as the sweet
spot is the ability

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:14.800
to run what some analysts
call translytical data

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:17.920
because the idea behind HANA
is that you do not move data

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.970
around, and that you can
run aggregations on the fly,

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:25.960
and not having to move data
around or persist aggregations.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.390
And then this says,
we actually use

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:31.330
it to run both
transactional and analytics

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:32.620
in that same appliance.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.800
So you can do transactional
and analytics.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:38.020
By having the analytics,
I mean not just

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.690
aggregations as I
just said, but also,

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:42.040
any of the advanced pieces.

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.280
We have machine learning.

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:47.080
We have predictive
algorithms, geospatial graph.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:50.260
You can even have a document
store on top of HANA

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:51.730
in the same platform.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:54.850
So literally, you do not
need to move data around.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:57.113
So that's why you're
asking, well, there's

00:12:57.113 --> 00:12:58.030
a lot that you can do.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:12:59.950
And yes, that is
exactly the sweet spot,

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:03.783
is having a lot of possibilities
out of the same piece of data.

00:13:03.783 --> 00:13:04.450
JON FOUST: Nice.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:05.890
And I'm actually
kind of curious.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:08.420
On the flip side, what
wouldn't you use it for?

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:10.003
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well,
I wouldn't use it

00:13:10.003 --> 00:13:12.760
if you only need a single
capability because it sounds

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:15.620
like an overkill, and if
you're going to run small data

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:17.390
sets because it's
also an overkill.

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:22.090
So the smallest unit that
we have is 32 gigs of RAM.

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:25.960
And I have that on my massive
computer that you saw earlier.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.120
But I'm writing
that on my laptop.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:30.700
But I ran that because
yesterday, for example, I

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.220
