00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:23.860
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well,
you technically

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:28.540
do not have a limit, because
it can scale up and scale out.

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:29.890
MARK MANDEL: Ooh, tell us more.

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:33.130
LUCIA SUBATIN: So you can make
a server bigger, add more RAM.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:35.750
Or you can make a
server clusters,

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:37.040
add friends do that server.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:41.355
So you can continue adding
data to your ecosystem of HANA

00:22:41.355 --> 00:22:41.855
servers.

00:22:41.855 --> 00:22:42.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:44.080
LUCIA SUBATIN: As long
as they can scale.

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:45.430
MARK MANDEL: And if
you're scaling out,

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:46.445
I know it's an
in-memory database,

00:22:46.445 --> 00:22:48.487
I know you were mentioning
transactional support.

00:22:48.487 --> 00:22:50.140
Are there any
limitations around that?

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:52.150
Or what sort of
scaling up strategies

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:53.860
can you use with SAP HANA?

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.740
LUCIA SUBATIN: Well, it depends
on how you're clustering

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:57.933
or segregating the data.

00:22:57.933 --> 00:22:58.600
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:01.810
LUCIA SUBATIN: But one
thing that I should mention,

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.120
is that HANA does
have the ability

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:07.197
to recognize that data doesn't
need to be in memory as well.

00:23:07.197 --> 00:23:08.280
MARK MANDEL: Oh, OK, cool.

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.655
LUCIA SUBATIN: So there is
something called data tiering.

00:23:10.655 --> 00:23:14.560
And you can choose to have the
data that is more commonly used

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:19.510
in memory, and the rest have it
like near memory, like in disc,

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:20.150
for example.

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:22.090
So that is one
thing, you not need

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:25.215
to have all of the columns
in a table loaded in memory.

00:23:25.215 --> 00:23:26.590
And actually, the
platform itself

00:23:26.590 --> 00:23:29.920
is going to unload data
that you're not using.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:33.250
So you as a developer, do not
really need to care about that.

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:35.290
One thing you may need
to care about that

00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:38.530
is, data causing latency,
because you're pulling data

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:42.280
from different physical nodes,
so you have network there.

00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:45.040
But it will depend a
lot on the use case

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:46.718
and how you're
partitioning the table.

00:23:46.718 --> 00:23:48.510
You can choose how you
partition the table.

