00:27:46.697 --> 00:27:48.780
MARK MANDEL: I'd been aware
of Google image search

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:50.520
for a really long
time, but I didn't

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:52.170
realize we had an
API that essentially

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:53.440
allowed you to do that.

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:55.070
So if you're not
familiar, basically it

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:56.820
means if you've got
an image, so if you've

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:59.443
got like a PNG or a JPEG
or something like that.

00:27:59.443 --> 00:28:00.860
And you're thinking
to yourself, I

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:02.640
wonder if somebody else
is hosting this somewhere

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:03.307
on the internet?

00:28:03.307 --> 00:28:05.935
Or where are all the
places this image is used,

00:28:05.935 --> 00:28:07.560
but you want to do
it programmatically,

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:11.190
the Cloud Vision API has
a detecting web entities

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:14.550
and pages API that enables
you to basically plug

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:17.850
in the content for that
image, base64 encoded.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:19.290
And then it'll
scour the internet

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:21.570
and send you back a response
basically giving you

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:24.780
URLs to say how much it
matches, as well as a score

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:27.015
to think of it how close
it believes that image is.

00:28:27.015 --> 00:28:28.140
It's actually kind of neat.

00:28:28.140 --> 00:28:29.550
I didn't know it did
this, so I'm actually

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:30.810
kind of excited about it.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.393
